-- title:
--   Galois-invariant elements of ℤ[ζ_q] are rational integers
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $K = \mathrm{CyclotomicField}\ q\ \mathbb{Q}$ be Mathlib's cyclotomic field of $q$-th roots of unity over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $c \in K$ be an element lying in `zetaSubring q`, the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by the single element `zetaQ q`, the distinguished primitive $q$-th root of unity supplied by the cyclotomic-extension structure on $K$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; thus $c$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-polynomial expression in $\zeta_q$. Assume further that $c$ is fixed by every $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $K$, i.e. $\sigma(c) = c$ for all $\sigma : K \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[\mathbb{Q}]} K$. The conclusion is that $c$ is the image of a rational integer: there exists $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ \mathbb{Z}\ K\ (m) = c$, that is, $c = m \cdot 1_K$.
--
--   This is the usual Galois-descent statement for cyclotomic integers: an element of $\mathbb{Z}[\zeta_q]$ invariant under $\mathrm{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_q)/\mathbb{Q})$ lies in $\mathbb{Z}$. It is used in the construction of a modular form of level $\Gamma_1$ with integral $q$-expansion and leading coefficient $1$ out of products of Siegel units, where a cyclotomic coefficient is shown to be invariant and hence rational integral.
-- source:
